00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.070
PROFESSOR: So once you
have the batteries out,

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.352
they go in the
back of the camera.

00:00:26.352 --> 00:00:29.020
This spot here, if you notice
a little notch is there,

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.470
just make notches on the
battery, on the bottom of it,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:33.850
match up with the notches here.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.220
And then just slide
it into place.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.670
There should be a push
button on the larger battery.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.422
Make sure that's on the bottom.

00:00:40.422 --> 00:00:42.130
If that's on the
bottom, it will match up

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.947
with the notches on the back.

00:00:44.947 --> 00:00:46.405
And it should just
snap into place.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.850
I think it goes in
the other way, yep.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.347
And you just got to
kind of feel it out.

00:00:57.347 --> 00:00:58.555
There you go, until it stops.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:05.660
There you go.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.890
You guys are
already on the tape.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.260
All right, once you
have the battery in,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.930
there's this little
wheel right here.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.930
If you could just push
the green button in on it

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.865
and slide it to on.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.360
And once that happens, you
should be in tape mode.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.020
The green light should be at the
very top of the three choices.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.850
Excellent.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:41.370
All right, now, you guys should
all have cassettes, tapes.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:46.685
To open the cassette, or
where you put the cassette in,

00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:48.810
you just see this little
thing on the bottom, where

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.320
it says open eject,
just slide it forward

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.620
and pull the cover open.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.570
And it will pop
out automatically.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.790
And then you just have
to put the cassette in.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.960
Make sure that the cassette
is face up when you put it in.

00:02:07.222 --> 00:02:08.074
AUDIENCE: Like this?

00:02:08.074 --> 00:02:08.699
PROFESSOR: Yep.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.410
Just like-- oh no, sorry.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.270
AUDIENCE: Oh, so
like it was before?

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:12.525
PROFESSOR: Yep.

00:02:12.525 --> 00:02:12.780
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.155
PROFESSOR: And then if you
just push that down, it should

00:02:15.155 --> 00:02:17.450
go back in automatically.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:18.080
Cool.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.520
And then you can close the--

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:32.780
and if you push that down,
it will go in automatically.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.340
And then you can
just close this.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.630
Everybody there?

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.070
Sweet.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.480
Now, on the actual lens hood,
some of them might be mixed up.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.880
But it's either on your
left or your right side.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.590
There's a little switch.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.600
It says close and open.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.460
If you could just
push that to open,

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.550
just so you can see stuff when
you turn the camera on or look

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.190
through the viewfinder,
or the LCD screen.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.980
Now, there are two
ways of doing this.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.998
Right now, I'm just going to
run you through the LCD screens.

00:03:16.998 --> 00:03:19.290
So it's right here on the
left hand side of the camera.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:23.040
If you can just open that up.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.280
You can also close this,
look through the viewfinder.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.500
There's something right
underneath the viewfinder

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.810
called the diopter.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.660
So if you see a
blurry image, you

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.070
can just push that to the side.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.150
Try to sharpen it.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.210
Sometimes with the LCD screens,
focus is hard to gauge.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:45.410
So the viewfinder
sometimes is a better gauge

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.590
of whether or not
things are in focus.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:51.230
So underneath the
viewfinder, again, there's

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.580
this little switch.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.700
If it looks blurry, just
play with the switch

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.095
until it becomes clearer.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.510
Again, if you guys
look at the LCD panel

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.010
when you open it up,
on the bottom right,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.340
there's a little button
called the P menu,

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.710
which stands for personal menu.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.987
The menu on this, the regular
menu, is pretty extensive.

00:04:12.987 --> 00:04:14.570
And there are a lot
of things in there

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:18.540
that you don't really need or
that you'll never really use.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.490
So if you just hit
the P Menu now,

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:26.150
you'll have two pages open up
that you can scroll through.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.340
And what I've done is
I consolidated the menu

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.350
into the most relevant choices
that you guys will probably

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.890
run into when you're
out there shooting.

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:38.610
The top left is
the regular menu.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.540
So if you ever want to get
more like advanced and in depth

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.970
into what the camera
can do, the top

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.850
left, just go through
the menu choices there.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:50.820
Right now it's pretty cursory as
far as what we're going to do.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.840
So as you can see, you have
the audio channel one level,

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.330
audio channel two
level, those buttons.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.420
A lot of people, they
make the mistake of they

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.940
try to record audio,
they'll go to the volume,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.810
which is really controlling
just the headphone volume.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.460
So sometimes people come back
be like, the levels are too low.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.890
And it's like, you
have to go through.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:16.820
Inside the camera case, there's
a little [INAUDIBLE] adaptor,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.340
which allows you to hook up
better shotgun microphones,

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.420
so you get better sound.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.610
So those are the two
audio level channels

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:28.190
that you're going to be
using or playing with,

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:29.910
instead of just the volume.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:31.490
That's why they're up there.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.960
The zebra, which is right
there next to audio channel two

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:39.060
level, the zebra is helpful.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.660
I think you can set it
at either a 70 or 100.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:45.710
And basically it tells you
when something is overexposed,

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.990
you'll see little zebra
stripes on the screen.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.070
So say you're playing
with exposure.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.290
And you're worried about
whether or not like you've

00:05:54.290 --> 00:06:00.690
hit the ceiling of what the
camera can actually record.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:03.680
Basically, you want
to explain IREs

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:05.405
or is it too much in depth?

00:06:05.405 --> 00:06:07.100
PROFESSOR 2: Well,
yeah, remember

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.250
I talked about exposure
and the aperture.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:15.260
In video, the exposure is
measured in units called

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.440
IRE units, which stands for--

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.665
PROFESSOR: International
Radio Engineers.

00:06:20.665 --> 00:06:22.040
PROFESSOR 2: Yeah,
it's basically

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:25.670
a standard that was
created 50 years ago

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.890
that measures the brightness
or the exposure of an image.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.100
And if you go past
100 IRE units,

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.780
you're not going
to record anything.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:34.530
That's basically where
you hit pure white.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.400
And adding light just
gives you more white.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:38.700
PROFESSOR: It's just blown out.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:43.280
So if you go into the editing
room and you try to bring some

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.960
detail back into
that, you can't.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.880
Because there's nothing there.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.380
PROFESSOR 2: So
the zebra stripes

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.430
tell you when you are either
approaching or exceeding

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.392
that threshold.

00:06:54.392 --> 00:06:56.600
PROFESSOR: And if you guys
want to just hit the zebra

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.720
button right now, because
it's one of those things

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:05.540
that you probably use, just
hit zebra on the touch screen.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.400
And if you notice,
there's three options.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.910
There's off, 70, and 100.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:13.580
What I would recommend is
when you use the zebra,

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.470
I would set it to 100.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:17.210
Because that's the
absolute threshold.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.580
I mean, that's the
highest you can go.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.620
When you see zebras
on 100, then you

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.195
may want to lower your exposure.

00:07:25.195 --> 00:07:26.570
And if you hit
that little button

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:29.720
on the bottom right of the
screen, the turn around button,

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:31.505
we'll get back to
the personal menu.

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:40.520
Now, after zebra, there is
the white balance option.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.020
White balance is
one of those things

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.460
that you want to
make sure you do,

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:47.780
but after you set your
exposure, after you've framed up

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:50.990
your image, after you focused.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:53.088
But it is important to do.

00:07:53.088 --> 00:07:54.630
It's all about the
color temperature.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.980
And if you don't white
balance correctly,

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:59.810
your image could look too--

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:04.248
like have this really ugly tint
of blue, or tint of orange,

00:08:04.248 --> 00:08:04.790
or something.

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:06.500
You want to make
sure that the color

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:11.930
temperature is represented
correctly within your camera.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.900
And if you guys look at the
right side of the camera,

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:19.040
toward the front, you'll
see a little round button

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.908
called the Assign button.

00:08:21.908 --> 00:08:23.450
What I've done with
all these cameras

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:28.610
is I've already programmed
one push white balance

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:29.970
to the Assign button.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.850
So instead of having
to go through the menu

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.850
options, whenever you
want to white balance,

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.530
you can just press
the Assign button.

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:38.400
And it will white
balance for you.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.850
So it's just a little
shortcut that all the cameras

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:45.440
should be able to access.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.598
When you are white balancing,
whenever you're going out

00:08:48.598 --> 00:08:51.140
on a shoot, try to make sure
you have a white piece of paper.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.420
That's probably the
simplest way to do it.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.030
If you're working with
somebody, just to have them

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:58.790
stand in front of the camera.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:04.040
Zoom so you you're filling the
frame with about at least 80%

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:05.900
of the white paper.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:07.820
Press the Assign button.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:10.520
There should be a blinking--

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.770
should be a blinking symbol.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.860
And once it stops blinking,
you're properly white balanced.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.540
PROFESSOR 2: So just to give
you a little more background

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:17.810
on that.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:20.570
Basically what you're doing when
you hold up that piece of paper

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:24.710
is you're saying to the camera,
OK, camera, this is white.

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:25.730
Make this white.

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:29.270
And whatever the kind
of color of light

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:34.370
you have shining on that
paper affects how the paper is

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:35.540
going to read to the camera.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:40.490
These fluorescent lights
look very, very different

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.620
compared to the
daylight outside.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.120
You can actually
see it in your eye.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:45.380
Our eyes are great at adjusting.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.130
But if you stare at
the light and then you

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:49.760
look down at the
exterior, you can

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.680
see that it's a little blue.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:55.340
So you put the paper under
whatever light you're filming.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:56.260
You frame it up.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.010
And you push that
button, which is saying,

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:01.400
OK, make that image white.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.980
Balance all the
colors in the sensor,

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.660
so that comes out white.

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:09.050
And then all the
other colors will be

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:10.370
relatively true and accurate.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:12.850
PROFESSOR: Because white
contains all the other colors.

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:14.767
It's a conglomeration
of all the other colors.

00:10:14.767 --> 00:10:17.300
Like if you can represent
white accurately,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.940
then all the other colors will
be represented accurately as

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.280
well.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:23.030
Right next to white
balance is spot focus,

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:25.700
which is kind of nifty.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:29.960
Basically, if you hit
Spot Focus, just press it,

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:31.940
you'll open up
this little screen.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:36.020
And if you press something
on the touch screen,

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:40.860
it will focus that
object or that person.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:43.040
So you may want to like--

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.450
if you guys notice on the back
of the camera, on the right,

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:52.130
there is a little lever, which
allows you to zoom in and out.

00:10:55.690 --> 00:11:03.340
Everybody-- what you may want
to do is just zoom in, go

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.350
a little--

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:08.380
not too much telephoto.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.390
Zoom into something
and try the spot focus

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:12.460
and see how it works.

00:11:16.231 --> 00:11:20.835
See how it adjusts after a
few seconds so it's in focus?

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:39.300
So if you just press something
with the spot focus enabled,

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.070
press something on the screen.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:43.815
And after a few seconds,
it should drift into focus.

00:11:51.134 --> 00:11:53.371
Is it working?

00:11:53.371 --> 00:11:54.640
It's working for me.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:06.150
The best way to judge is if
you zoom into something, so

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:07.913
go telephoto for something.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:20.070
Yeah, if you just wait a few
seconds, it should kick in.

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:33.354
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:33.354 --> 00:12:34.780
PROFESSOR: Oh, OK.

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:37.390
If you see the zebra, that
means that that's overexposed.

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:39.640
It's not a huge deal
here, because I mean,

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.020
it's not a detail shot.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.660
If it's something that you--

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:44.560
if you want to keep the
detail in something,

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:48.280
like whether it's a person or
an object, if you see the zebra,

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:51.190
you may want to bring
the exposure down, just

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:52.510
so you can keep that detail.

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:54.280
And it will record
it accurately.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:56.627
But for something like
the edge of a table,

00:12:56.627 --> 00:12:57.460
it's not a big deal.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:59.600
Because there's no
real detail in it.

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:05.620
If you guys go back
to the P menu--

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:08.648
and I'll try to speed this up.

00:13:08.648 --> 00:13:09.940
PROFESSOR 2: We're doing great.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:12.370
PROFESSOR: Oh, OK.

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:16.270
Back to the P menu, if you
scroll down to the second page,

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:20.770
you'll see peaking, histogram,
study shot, exposure lever,

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:22.840
and spot meter.

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:25.120
Is everybody with me?

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.040
Sometimes with the
LCD screens, it's

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.370
really hard to gauge focus.

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:31.728
So there's something called
the peaking function,

00:13:31.728 --> 00:13:33.520
which is the top left
of the second screen.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:36.807
If you guys want
to just hit that,

00:13:36.807 --> 00:13:38.140
you'll see there's four options.

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:41.320
There's off, white,
red, and yellow.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:42.740
And what peaking does--

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:45.700
and by the way, you can't
have peaking and zebra

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:46.990
at the same time.

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:49.580
They can't both be
enabled at the same time.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.280
So what you probably
want to do is

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:54.430
if you're going to be in the
same area, set your exposure

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:56.350
first with the zebra.

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:58.390
Make sure that
nothing's overexposed.

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:01.670
And if you want to use the
peaking, go to that after.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:05.050
But with these LCD screens,
where it's really hard

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:08.740
to gauge focus,
you have the option

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:11.080
of surrounding people
or objects that

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:13.090
are in focus with
the color white,

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:17.890
like a white outline, a red
outline, or a yellow outline.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:19.933
So anything that's
in focus, you can

00:14:19.933 --> 00:14:21.100
choose one of those options.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:25.774
And the person or
thing in focus will

00:14:25.774 --> 00:14:31.840
have a slight trace of white,
red, or yellow around them.

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:34.190
And again, with these
screens, whether or not

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.850
sometimes they're smudged
or the resolution just

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:39.010
isn't very good,
sometimes that's

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.460
a good option to have available.

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:46.007
Because, like Chris was saying,
if something's out of focus

00:14:46.007 --> 00:14:47.590
and you bring it
into the editing room

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:49.930
and it's blown up even
bigger, you can really

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:51.280
tell that something's soft.

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:53.840
So peaking is one
of those things

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:55.840
that you may want to pull
out from time to time,

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:58.225
just to make sure
that you're in focus.

00:14:58.225 --> 00:15:01.870
PROFESSOR 2: And one
other note about focus.

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:03.460
Sometimes you'll
find, and today is

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.830
one of those days, when it's
really, really bright out,

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:08.500
the screen may not
be your best option

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:10.452
in terms of seeing the image.

00:15:10.452 --> 00:15:12.160
If you close the
screen, you can actually

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:13.327
look through the viewfinder.

00:15:13.327 --> 00:15:16.480
And then your eye is shadowed.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:18.670
So even in very, very
bright conditions,

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:22.060
you can see pretty accurately
what image you're getting.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.490
And that can be very helpful,
especially for focus.

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:25.157
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:15:27.265 --> 00:15:29.640
PROFESSOR 2: Unfortunately,
you cannot use the viewfinder

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:30.780
and the screen at the same time.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:31.822
PROFESSOR: No, you can't.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:41.280
After peeking, there's
the histogram, which

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.070
measures your exposure levels.

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:46.920
I think to the left of the
screen is your black levels.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:48.600
And to the right
is your highlights.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:52.410
So if you really want to have
a nice balance in contrast

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:56.220
to your frame, the histogram
is a good thing to have open.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:59.610
It doesn't take up too much
space on the LCD screen.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:03.270
But we can talk about
that more later on.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:07.230
Steady shot, whenever
you're not using a tripod,

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:10.530
make sure that steady
shot is enabled.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:14.640
Because it over
compensates, like

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:16.710
if your camera's
going one way, if it's

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:18.990
a little shaky with
your hand held work,

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:20.818
steady shot just
over compensates

00:16:20.818 --> 00:16:22.110
with the movement of the frame.

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:29.350
So it just stays steadier than
if you didn't have it enabled.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:33.670
Exposure lever, it's
not really a big deal.

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:39.010
Spot meter, a spot meter
is kind of like the--

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:41.401
was it the focus, Peter?

00:16:41.401 --> 00:16:42.880
PROFESSOR 2: Spot meter is--

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:44.830
PROFESSOR: No, but
what was the other one?

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:45.850
AUDIENCE: Spot focus?

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:46.975
PROFESSOR: Spot focus, yes.

00:16:46.975 --> 00:16:50.210
It's like spot focus, except
it works with exposure.

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:55.957
So if you guys want
to hit spot meter,

00:16:55.957 --> 00:16:57.790
this is good for like
interviews or anything

00:16:57.790 --> 00:17:01.150
with people, especially if
say there's somebody sitting

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:02.770
right in front of
a window and you're

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:04.908
getting all kinds of
zebras in the window,

00:17:04.908 --> 00:17:05.950
because it's overexposed.

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:07.783
But you want to make
sure the person sitting

00:17:07.783 --> 00:17:10.300
in front of the window
is properly exposed.

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:14.272
If you, again, just like spot
focus, if you hit that person,

00:17:14.272 --> 00:17:15.730
or whatever it is
want to make sure

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.740
is properly exposed
on the screen,

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:21.130
it will adjust your
exposure automatically.

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:26.410
So it tries to maintain proper
exposure on whatever you

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:29.510
touch on the screen.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:31.940
And that's good in a
jam if you ever need it.

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:44.880
As far as the basic
functions that

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:49.230
are on the outside of the
camera, that are on the shell,

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:51.720
there are two, well
actually, three ways

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:55.710
to zoom, to go from
wide to telephoto.

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:58.530
One is the ring.

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:02.820
But what I would
recommend everybody

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:06.510
do is you see on the left
hand side of the camera

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:09.810
at the front, right
behind the ring?

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:10.560
AUDIENCE: Show me.

00:18:13.225 --> 00:18:13.892
PROFESSOR: Here.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:23.510
Right above where it says
focus, there are three options.

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:26.830
You can go all the way
to the front for zoom.

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:29.860
You can bring it toward
the middle for auto focus.

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:32.420
Or if you switch it all
the way to the back,

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:33.940
you can have manual focus.

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:35.470
AUDIENCE: Hold still, please.

00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:37.390
Show me on top.

00:18:37.390 --> 00:18:39.208
Thank you.

00:18:39.208 --> 00:18:42.932
PROFESSOR 2: Are you zooming in?

00:18:42.932 --> 00:18:45.140
PROFESSOR: Think it might
be in the light, all right.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:46.723
So if you go all the
way back, I would

00:18:46.723 --> 00:18:48.950
recommend keeping that
on manual focus, which

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:52.202
is all the way toward the back.

00:18:52.202 --> 00:18:55.840
Because you only have one ring.

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:58.900
And you want to make sure that
that's freed up for focus.

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:00.760
Because there are
already two ways to zoom.

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:05.210
On the LCD screen itself, on the
left hand side of the screen,

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:07.900
you'll see that
there's a W and a T.

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:11.440
So if you want to zoom
out, you'd hit the W.

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:13.510
And it's kind of slow.

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:16.270
If you want to zoom in, you
hit the T for telephoto.

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:20.530
If that's too slow
for you, there's

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:24.190
also a snap zoom on the back
right of the camera, which

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:25.970
is this lever right here.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:28.300
AUDIENCE: Can you say
that one more time for me?

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:29.410
PROFESSOR: Which part?

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:31.480
AUDIENCE: What is the snap zoom?

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:34.100
PROFESSOR: The snap zoom is on
the back right of the camera.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:38.770
And if the zoom on the LCD goes
a little too slowly for you,

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:43.190
I would recommend the snap zoom,
which is pressure sensitive.

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:46.600
So it doesn't mean that you
can't control your zoom.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:50.400
It just means if you want
the option of going faster,

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:51.783
you have it.

00:19:51.783 --> 00:19:53.200
So if you guys
want to play around

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:56.740
with the zoom on the back
right, again, it's labeled,

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:01.270
so W and T. So W for
wide, T for telephoto.

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:05.800
So just play around
with that for a second.

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:06.910
I'm zooming right now.

00:20:18.418 --> 00:20:20.710
AUDIENCE: I'm just going to
let that out of your shirt.

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:21.445
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:20:21.445 --> 00:20:22.320
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:22.987
PROFESSOR: Sure.

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:30.240
So the top left,
now that it's set

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:34.650
on manual focus,
that switch, you

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.260
can now focus with
the focus ring.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:42.180
So again, the best way to play
around with focus is use either

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:46.570
the zoom tools on the LCD or
on the back of the camera,

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:51.330
and maybe zoom in to one of the
chairs or something that's--

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:56.130
one of the cases or zoom
in fairly closely, and just

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.123
play with the focus ring.

00:20:58.123 --> 00:20:59.790
So you get a good
sense of how it works.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:26.060
I'm going to focus on
your headphones, Brian.

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:29.830
I'm going to focus those there.

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:33.050
Excellent.

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:37.720
All right, last
thing is exposure.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:40.090
If you guys look
toward the front

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:47.530
of the camera, on the bottom,
on the left, there's a lever.

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:51.140
You move that up and
down to control exposure.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:54.490
So again, that's the F
stop or the aperture.

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:59.080
So to make the image
brighter or darker,

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:00.880
I want you guys play
around with that.

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:01.910
Tell me if it's working.

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:03.850
If it's not working,
there's an easy fix.

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:07.706
AUDIENCE: Can you just
turn the camera towards me

00:22:07.706 --> 00:22:08.410
so I can that?

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:09.160
Point to it again.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:11.095
PROFESSOR: This is
the exposure lever.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:23.310
Is that working for everybody?

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:25.290
Because sometimes you have to
press the button next to it.

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:26.123
It's kind of quirky.

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:37.100
There should also be a bar
on the bottom of the screen,

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.500
as your exposing.

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:51.420
Is everybody comfortable
with the basic functions

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:52.283
of the camera?

00:22:52.283 --> 00:22:53.700
PROFESSOR 2: And
obviously, you're

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:56.260
going to gain a
lot of experience

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:58.120
very quickly with how
these things work.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:01.410
So if you're a little
bit, you know, obviously

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:03.330
at C with some of them,
don't worry too much.

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:07.960
AUDIENCE: How do you
turn the volume down,

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:09.550
so it's not beeping
all the time?

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:10.900
PROFESSOR: Oh, that's easy.

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:13.027
It's in the-- oh,
do you want to?

00:23:13.027 --> 00:23:14.110
PROFESSOR 2: No, go ahead.

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:16.270
PROFESSOR: It's in the menu.

00:23:16.270 --> 00:23:19.490
So that's one of those that--

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:21.430
let me turn mine
back on actually--

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:22.760
that's in the main menu.

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:30.730
So if you hit the P menu, and
on the top left, the menu.

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:33.520
I believe it's in camera set.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:38.270
So if camera set is already
highlighted, press OK.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:40.340
And scroll down.

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:42.440
And you should see
beep eventually.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:48.165
I can hear that beeping.

00:23:48.165 --> 00:23:49.540
Oh, actually it's
in the toolbox.

00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:52.850
AUDIENCE: Toolbox?

00:23:55.888 --> 00:23:58.430
PROFESSOR: Yeah, you're going
to have to scroll down a while.

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:09.842
Keep going.

00:24:09.842 --> 00:24:10.550
AUDIENCE: Volume?

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:11.150
PROFESSOR: No.

00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:12.500
That's for the headphones.

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:13.760
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:15.440
PROFESSOR: And beep is close.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:17.580
BP is-- there it is.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:18.420
AUDIENCE: All right.

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:20.628
PROFESSOR 2: So you can see
that these menus are very

00:24:20.628 --> 00:24:22.753
complicated, the main menu.

00:24:22.753 --> 00:24:23.670
AUDIENCE: There we go.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:24.490
Thank you.

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:26.860
PROFESSOR: No problem.

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:28.680
Anything else?

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:30.780
Questions?

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:31.820
Solutions?

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:32.385
No?

00:24:32.385 --> 00:24:35.400
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:36.360
PROFESSOR: Sure.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.348
So if you have somebody
else with you--

00:24:38.348 --> 00:24:40.140
I mean, you could
probably do it yourself--

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:45.420
hold a white sheet of paper in
front of your camera and make

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:46.695
sure that your frame is--

00:24:46.695 --> 00:24:50.590
PROFESSOR 2: Here,
I'll be your model.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:53.400
So again, what I
would do is make sure

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:56.080
that the light that is
hitting your subject,

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:57.780
if the subject is
me, is also hitting

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:01.840
the paper, which it is now.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:03.840
AUDIENCE: So should I
zoom into the white paper?

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:05.010
PROFESSOR 2: Zoom
into the paper, yeah.

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:06.927
PROFESSOR: And make sure
it fills at least 80%

00:25:06.927 --> 00:25:08.865
of the frame.

00:25:08.865 --> 00:25:11.093
AUDIENCE: So every
time you change your--

00:25:11.093 --> 00:25:12.135
PROFESSOR: Your lighting.

00:25:12.135 --> 00:25:13.290
AUDIENCE: Oh, just your--

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:15.600
PROFESSOR 2: Just
the lighting, yeah.

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:18.180
This would be a difficult
room, because the light

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:20.875
by the windows is very different
from the light over here.

00:25:20.875 --> 00:25:21.750
So you might adjust--

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:23.370
PROFESSOR: If you press
the assign button,

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:24.870
you'll see a little blinking--
it looks like a flower almost.

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:26.453
PROFESSOR 2: If the
blinds were closed

00:25:26.453 --> 00:25:28.230
and it was all
fluorescence, once you

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:30.632
adjusted for these
lights, you would be fine.

00:25:30.632 --> 00:25:31.590
PROFESSOR: There it is.

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:34.240
Once that stops blinking, you'll
be properly light balanced.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:36.820
PROFESSOR 2: Yeah, exactly.

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:40.020
And once you go outside today to
shoot, once you adjust it once,

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:41.160
you'll be OK.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:43.743
Then you'd have to
probably adjust it again,

00:25:43.743 --> 00:25:44.910
as long as you stay outside.

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:46.618
AUDIENCE: Oh, wait,
it's blinking slower.

00:25:46.618 --> 00:25:47.746
It was blinking faster.

00:25:47.746 --> 00:25:49.450
Then it was blinking slower.

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:50.718
PROFESSOR: Let me see.

00:25:54.390 --> 00:25:56.085
It should stop blinking.

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:02.820
PROFESSOR 2: It's not working?

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:04.112
PROFESSOR: It's still blinking.

00:26:04.112 --> 00:26:05.682
PROFESSOR 2: Uh-oh.

00:26:05.682 --> 00:26:06.670
PROFESSOR: All right.

00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:20.860
All right, it should be OK.

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.170
It shouldn't be blinking.

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:28.020
But the color temperature
looked to change,

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:31.035
so it seemed to change.

00:26:31.035 --> 00:26:35.087
All right, now, one more quick
thing before we head out.

00:26:35.087 --> 00:26:37.170
PROFESSOR 2: Do you want
to talk about the tripod?

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:39.010
PROFESSOR: Yeah, that's
what we're going to do.

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:41.010
So now everybody has tape
in the camera, right?

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:43.510
Because once we put the plates
on the bottom of the cameras,

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:47.670
it's going to be impossible to
take any tape out or put it in.

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:48.783
Everybody has tape in?

00:26:48.783 --> 00:26:50.700
PROFESSOR 2: One
disadvantage with this design

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:53.290
is that if you are
shooting with a tripod,

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:57.250
you have to take it off the
tripod in order to change tape.

00:26:57.250 --> 00:26:58.830
That's how they
compromised and made

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:03.043
a nice camera that's so small.

00:27:03.043 --> 00:27:05.210
But anyway, these tripods
are about twice the weight

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:07.680
of the camera and
very cumbersome.

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:11.060
So I know you'll
want to not use them.

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:14.210
And in some
situations, that's OK.

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:16.820
But I also think it's very
important to know about them

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:18.590
and get used to using them.

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:24.770
Because for a lot of
situations, interviews,

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:28.100
elegant architectural
shots, beauty shots,

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:31.340
it is important to use them
and to know how to use them.

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:33.268
PROFESSOR: Anything that
involves control, as

00:27:33.268 --> 00:27:34.310
much control as possible.

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:35.990
PROFESSOR 2: Exactly, if you're
not shooting something that's

00:27:35.990 --> 00:27:37.790
a lot of action or motion.

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:38.825
PROFESSOR: Right.

00:27:38.825 --> 00:27:40.450
PROFESSOR 2: So
anyway, go ahead, Dave.

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:43.030
PROFESSOR: So if
you guys could just

00:27:43.030 --> 00:27:49.990
grab the tripod bags that are
right there against the wall.

00:27:49.990 --> 00:27:52.330
There is a plate on top.

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:57.370
And you want to release it
from the head of the tripod.

00:27:57.370 --> 00:28:01.990
So there's a little lever
on the right hand side.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:07.570
Or there's a little latch.

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:10.390
And you just want
to turn that latch.

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:14.230
And then on the left hand
side, there is a yellow button

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:15.490
that you want to press in.

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:19.240
And then your plate should
slide right off the top.

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:20.923
PROFESSOR 2: If you push that--

00:28:20.923 --> 00:28:22.090
make sure that that's loose.

00:28:26.290 --> 00:28:27.940
Yeah, it's this way.

00:28:30.762 --> 00:28:32.470
So that goes on the
bottom of the camera.

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:34.000
PROFESSOR: Once
that's off, you'll

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:37.120
notice that there's two holes
on the bottom of the camera.

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:40.960
Make sure that, if you
turn the plate over,

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:44.788
you'll see there's a word
lens and there's an arrow.

00:28:44.788 --> 00:28:46.330
Just make sure that
arrow is pointing

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:49.280
toward the front of the camera.

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:51.640
And then tighten up the screw.

00:28:54.730 --> 00:28:59.420
I wish-- yes, the
best thing to use

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:02.240
is like the flat side
of a key or a quarter,

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:04.790
and make sure that
screw is really tight.

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:08.180
Because that's basically if
you're tilting and panning,

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:11.510
that's what's keeping
your camera on the tripod.

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:13.812
And you don't want
it falling off.

00:29:13.812 --> 00:29:15.770
PROFESSOR 2: So the whole
purpose of this plate

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:17.930
is basically so
that you can quickly

00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:19.520
release the camera
from the tripod

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:21.408
and quickly put it
back on, in case

00:29:21.408 --> 00:29:23.450
you want to go handheld
in the middle of a shoot,

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:27.680
and then easily slide
it back onto the tripod.

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:29.630
And just one thing to note.

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:31.790
It's almost the
number one thing that

00:29:31.790 --> 00:29:33.825
happens when equipment
gets checked in and out

00:29:33.825 --> 00:29:35.450
is that people forget
to take the plate

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:37.100
off the bottom of the camera.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:40.490
The plate stays with the
tripod, not with the camera.

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:43.490
So you always have to remember
to unattach it and slide it

00:29:43.490 --> 00:29:47.790
back onto the tripod when
you return the equipment.

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:51.180
PROFESSOR: Now, if
you have the plate

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:53.190
on the bottom of
the camera and it's

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:56.223
the screw is really
tight on the bottom,

00:29:56.223 --> 00:29:57.390
make sure it's really tight.

00:29:57.390 --> 00:30:00.630
Again, the flat head
of a key or a quarter

00:30:00.630 --> 00:30:03.930
would work very, very well.

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:05.650
And it looks like
you're all set.

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:09.630
If you just from the
back slide it back

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:13.590
into the top of the
tripod head and then

00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:18.607
tighten that screw on the
right again, or the latch.

00:30:18.607 --> 00:30:20.690
Make sure it's really
tight, so the camera, again,

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:21.560
doesn't slide off.

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:32.680
PROFESSOR 2: No, actually,
I guess it's all right.

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:34.110
I tend to line that one up.

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:36.000
See that little brass--

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:37.380
AUDIENCE: Oh.

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:38.945
PROFESSOR: Now,
on the left side,

00:30:38.945 --> 00:30:40.320
there should be
a handle that you

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:45.030
can turn to adjust the fluidity
of the actual tripod head.

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:47.682
So if you want to have a
little more resistance,

00:30:47.682 --> 00:30:49.140
you can play with
that if you want.

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:51.272
A little less
resistance, play with it.

00:30:51.272 --> 00:30:52.980
PROFESSOR 2: You slide
it on from-- yeah.

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:56.730
So again, you push that
in and slide it on.

00:30:56.730 --> 00:30:58.295
Let go of that.

00:30:58.295 --> 00:31:00.420
Make sure that it's just
engaged exactly like that.

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:01.503
Now let go of the red one.

00:31:01.503 --> 00:31:04.760
And now lock it with that.

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:05.260
see?

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:06.370
So now it's nice and snug.

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:08.230
And then if you want
to take it off quickly,

00:31:08.230 --> 00:31:10.700
you always hold on to it, so
it doesn't go anywhere, right?

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:13.260
You just undo that one.

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:15.450
Push that button.

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:16.620
It goes on back to front.

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:19.230
So it comes off that
way, goes on that way.

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:22.233
This is just like
an emergency thing.

00:31:22.233 --> 00:31:23.400
So let's say this was blue--

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.260
PROFESSOR: But whenever
it's like unattended,

00:31:25.260 --> 00:31:27.052
make sure that this is
all the way forward,

00:31:27.052 --> 00:31:29.520
yeah, so it doesn't go anywhere.

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:31.500
But this is how much fluidity.

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:34.978
Also, right here, you can
adjust the fluidity too.

00:31:34.978 --> 00:31:35.520
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:36.103
PROFESSOR: OK?

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:46.690
PROFESSOR 2: This is
really like a basic thing.

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:48.940
Like the camera
could easily fall.

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:58.210
PROFESSOR: And I guess the last
thing would be just balancing

00:31:58.210 --> 00:31:59.770
it.

00:31:59.770 --> 00:32:01.840
PROFESSOR 2: I mean,
they're so light that it--

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:04.630
but yeah, you're right,
so that it doesn't

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:06.700
tilt forward or backward.

00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:10.450
PROFESSOR: No, I'm talking about
making sure that it's balanced,

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:11.770
like the level.

00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:14.380
PROFESSOR 2: Oh, level, yeah.

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:16.960
PROFESSOR: So there's a
little knob on the underneath.

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:22.630
If you turn that, you can loosen
the actual head from the legs.

00:32:22.630 --> 00:32:25.157
And yeah, just make
sure it's level there.

00:32:25.157 --> 00:32:25.990
And then tighten it.

00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:30.735
And whenever--

00:32:30.735 --> 00:32:32.360
AUDIENCE: Oh, should
I be holding this?

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:53.230
PROFESSOR 2: But anyway, so
this is just really up to you.

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:55.230
PROFESSOR: And just make
sure it's really tight.

00:33:01.535 --> 00:33:03.960
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:05.250
Can I just hold this?

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:06.930
PROFESSOR: Yeah,
it's probably best

00:33:06.930 --> 00:33:08.755
to hold the camera
when are you doing it.

00:33:08.755 --> 00:33:09.705
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK.

00:33:12.803 --> 00:33:14.470
PROFESSOR: One thing
you could try to do

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:18.662
is just nudge it with like
a closed fist or something.

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:28.144
AUDIENCE: Is that good?

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:32.206
PROFESSOR 2: That's the tilt.

00:33:32.206 --> 00:33:32.831
AUDIENCE: Tilt?

00:33:32.831 --> 00:33:34.110
Which one's [INAUDIBLE]?

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:34.785
PROFESSOR 2: It's this.

00:33:34.785 --> 00:33:36.202
It's just how much
you tighten it.

00:33:36.202 --> 00:33:37.355
So like you can tighten it.

00:33:37.355 --> 00:33:39.480
You don't want to tighten
it so much that you would

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:40.650
break the tripod obviously.

00:33:40.650 --> 00:33:43.020
But like this is loose.

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:46.060
This is tighter.

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:52.910
Then I think-- here, I think
this is the pan friction.

00:33:52.910 --> 00:34:00.190
So you can add friction
by turning that as well.

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:04.650
So you can both tilt and pan
at different rates of drag.

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:08.280
And you can mimic, as I said,
mimic a much heavier camera,

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:09.000
like that camera.

00:34:21.699 --> 00:34:22.719
OK, questions?

00:34:26.028 --> 00:34:27.070
PROFESSOR: Any questions?

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:32.030
AUDIENCE: What's the lever
near the light [INAUDIBLE]??

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:34.730
PROFESSOR: That is
for the head itself.

00:34:34.730 --> 00:34:38.168
I think that's if you're going
to be like taking it apart.

00:34:38.168 --> 00:34:41.425
AUDIENCE: Which
way should it be?

00:34:41.425 --> 00:34:43.050
PROFESSOR: It should
be pushed forward.

00:34:46.409 --> 00:34:50.940
PROFESSOR 2: OK, if everyone is
kind of at a comfortable place,

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:52.142
we'll take a short break.

00:34:52.142 --> 00:34:54.600
I mean, you're welcome to stay
here and keep playing around

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:55.267
with the camera.

00:34:55.267 --> 00:34:57.660
Or you can just relax.

00:34:57.660 --> 00:34:59.520
We'll come back in
five or 10 minutes.

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:03.780
And I'll talk a little bit about
the assignment, the exercise.

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:05.820
And then the rest of
the time we'll basically

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:09.210
go out and do a little filming.

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:11.460
I have eight separate
kinds of subjects,

00:35:11.460 --> 00:35:14.490
types of subjects, that I want
you to try to go and film.

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:16.980
And if you only film six
of them, that's fine.

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:20.640
This is not necessarily
a graded exercise.

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:22.590
But these are the
kinds of things

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:26.490
that I think would be great
for you to experience doing.

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:29.700
So film a landscape or
an architectural view

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:32.440
of an exterior of a building.

00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:36.600
So in other words, an
environmental shot.

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:41.280
Film an interior space, either
static or moving, panning,

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:44.750
going through the space,
revealing the space.

00:35:44.750 --> 00:35:45.990
You can go to Stata.

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:47.760
You can go to the hall.

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:50.421
You can just be creative.

00:35:50.421 --> 00:35:53.340
Explore an environment
with a shot.

00:35:53.340 --> 00:35:54.600
Film a portrait of a person.

00:35:57.240 --> 00:35:59.190
And we're not doing audio now.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:00.130
We're not doing sound.

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:01.255
We're not doing interviews.

00:36:01.255 --> 00:36:02.970
We're not worrying
about that yet.

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:06.750
Create a nice portrait
of a person visually.

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:08.820
Follow something.

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:10.170
And that could be handheld.

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:10.920
It could be a pan.

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:12.090
It could be both.

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:15.103
Just move the camera and
figure out a motivation

00:36:15.103 --> 00:36:16.020
for moving the camera.

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:18.153
Remember what we talked
about that when you're

00:36:18.153 --> 00:36:20.820
moving the camera, you should be
doing something with that move.

00:36:20.820 --> 00:36:23.820
You can either literally follow
a person or some other moving

00:36:23.820 --> 00:36:24.690
object.

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:26.880
You can reveal something.

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:29.480
So move the camera.

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:32.420
Film a close up of something.

00:36:32.420 --> 00:36:36.970
In other words, you know how
Brian, our camera person,

00:36:36.970 --> 00:36:41.830
was zooming in on Dave
as he showed the buttons?

00:36:41.830 --> 00:36:45.130
Film an activity and
illustrate something

00:36:45.130 --> 00:36:47.885
with the camera, probably
in a close up like that.

00:36:47.885 --> 00:36:49.510
But if you find some
other way to do it

00:36:49.510 --> 00:36:52.610
or some other shot that
tells that kind of story,

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:55.330
that's fine too.

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:58.300
Think about a way to employ
a foreground in a background

00:36:58.300 --> 00:36:59.980
element.

00:36:59.980 --> 00:37:02.200
So a person in the
foreground, something

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:05.710
in the background, two people,
whatever you might want to do.

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:13.078
And reveal something
with the camera.

00:37:13.078 --> 00:37:15.370
That could be the same as
the move the camera exercise.

00:37:15.370 --> 00:37:20.305
Again, if you're actually come
back with eight shots here.

00:37:20.305 --> 00:37:21.680
Reveal something
with the camera.

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:23.530
And then the last
thing, film something

00:37:23.530 --> 00:37:25.900
that you think is
a beautiful image.

00:37:25.900 --> 00:37:29.380
Create something that is at
least your attempt at making

00:37:29.380 --> 00:37:32.680
something that's beautiful.

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:33.430
Could be a person.

00:37:33.430 --> 00:37:34.400
Could be an image.

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:35.320
Could be a landscape.

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:36.153
Could be a close up.

00:37:36.153 --> 00:37:38.620
Could be one of
these other things.

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:41.590
But try to create something
aesthetically pleasing.

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:47.650
PROFESSOR: And whatever
you're panning,

00:37:47.650 --> 00:37:50.890
it's good to position
your body where

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:53.000
you'll be for the last frame.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:55.300
So if I'm walking
that way, you probably

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:58.270
want to position
your body like here.

00:37:58.270 --> 00:38:01.930
Because that way,
you're unwinding

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:03.730
rather than like
you're constantly

00:38:03.730 --> 00:38:05.140
stumbling with your feet.

00:38:05.140 --> 00:38:07.000
So find out what
your last frame is.

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:08.920
And try to position
yourself there.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:11.380
Because you're going to be
uncoiling your body rather

00:38:11.380 --> 00:38:13.840
than moving your feet.

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:16.000
But we'll try it a few times.

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:18.940
Do you want try it once
without recording it

00:38:18.940 --> 00:38:20.485
and just see how it goes?

00:38:20.485 --> 00:38:22.220
You can practice just tracking.

00:38:31.666 --> 00:38:34.630
AUDIENCE: So am I supposed
to shoot [INAUDIBLE]??

00:38:39.590 --> 00:38:42.164
AUDIENCE: No, so I'm
going to stand there.

00:38:42.164 --> 00:38:43.508
So [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:49.760
PROFESSOR: Oh, OK.

00:38:49.760 --> 00:38:53.720
AUDIENCE: Well, do we have
to use the spot focus?

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:55.970
PROFESSOR: Yeah, I mean, the
thing with the spot focus

00:38:55.970 --> 00:38:59.220
is it takes a few
seconds just to adjust.

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:02.690
So you may want to try
to just manually focus.

00:39:02.690 --> 00:39:07.700
Or you may want to just try to
focus as I get closer and then

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:09.620
worry about focus then.

00:39:09.620 --> 00:39:12.142
But also, when you're
panning, when I get closer,

00:39:12.142 --> 00:39:13.850
you could probably
tilt a little bit just

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:19.100
to try to keep like the same
composition, like medium shot.

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:20.930
Try to go with a medium shot.

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:22.445
It's going to be difficult.

00:39:22.445 --> 00:39:23.070
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:24.498
Yeah.

00:39:24.498 --> 00:39:25.790
AUDIENCE: What's a medium shot?

00:39:25.790 --> 00:39:27.230
PROFESSOR: So like from here up.

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:27.830
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:39:27.830 --> 00:39:30.440
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:32.660
AUDIENCE: My question
is because you're

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:36.290
going to have different-- if
you do the spot focus, then it's

00:39:36.290 --> 00:39:38.690
going to focus on you,
automatically focus on you,

00:39:38.690 --> 00:39:40.720
as you're walking?

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:45.430
PROFESSOR: Yeah, I mean, I think
it may take a couple of seconds

00:39:45.430 --> 00:39:47.930
to readjust with motion.

00:39:47.930 --> 00:39:51.490
So it might be, yeah, just
for like a few seconds.

00:39:51.490 --> 00:39:52.405
But you can try that.

00:39:52.405 --> 00:39:53.470
It would kind of cool.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:55.150
Give it a shot.

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:59.050
And you may want to just adjust
the drag on this a little bit.

00:39:59.050 --> 00:40:03.430
Because right now,
it's pretty tight.

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:04.875
Just give me the OK sign.

00:40:04.875 --> 00:40:05.500
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:39.302
I forgot to zoom in after--

00:40:39.302 --> 00:40:40.260
PROFESSOR: What's that?

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:42.120
AUDIENCE: I was
trying to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:45.670
I was so worried
about the frame,

00:40:45.670 --> 00:40:47.327
I forgot to actually zoom in.

00:40:47.327 --> 00:40:47.910
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:49.620
I mean, what you could do
is try to worry about one

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:51.720
thing at a time, like
worry about the framing.

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:54.630
Maybe try to keep
me in a medium shot.

00:40:54.630 --> 00:40:58.440
And then stuff like
focus, that'll take time

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:00.370
to juggle all things at once.

00:41:00.370 --> 00:41:08.310
So maybe just worry
about tracking my motion

00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:11.550
and keeping me in a medium
shot from the waist up.

00:41:13.507 --> 00:41:14.340
You didn't get the--

00:41:14.340 --> 00:41:16.260
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:17.220
PROFESSOR: Right.

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:18.810
So you're going
to have to adjust

00:41:18.810 --> 00:41:23.490
the focal length at some point,
just to keep me a medium shot.

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:24.270
So let's try that.

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:32.080
AUDIENCE: By adjusting
focal length, you mean?

00:41:32.080 --> 00:41:33.330
PROFESSOR: Zooming in and out.

00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:34.693
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:41:34.693 --> 00:41:36.860
PROFESSOR: You're possibly
going to have to zoom out

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:38.152
a little bit when I get closer.

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:13.968
AUDIENCE: Panning and zooming.

00:42:13.968 --> 00:42:16.010
PROFESSOR: And a little
bit of a tilt too, right?

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:17.793
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:42:17.793 --> 00:42:20.210
PROFESSOR: Do you guys want
to try some of the other shots

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:20.850
on the list?

00:42:20.850 --> 00:42:22.010
AUDIENCE: Can I
try one more time?

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:22.677
PROFESSOR: Sure.

00:42:22.677 --> 00:42:24.800
AUDIENCE: My first
attempt was a disaster.

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:25.670
PROFESSOR: Do want
the same thing?

00:42:25.670 --> 00:42:26.600
AUDIENCE: Yeah, just the same.

00:42:26.600 --> 00:42:29.180
PROFESSOR: OK, just give me
the OK sign when you're ready.

00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:34.600
And when you're
focusing on a person,

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:37.990
try to zoom in all the way
and look for like features

00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:40.147
on the person's face that
are really distinctive.

00:42:40.147 --> 00:42:41.605
Those are the best
places to focus.

00:42:46.510 --> 00:42:48.950
But I mean, you're going to
be out of focus at some point

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:49.450
anyway.

00:43:04.128 --> 00:43:05.420
AUDIENCE: OK, you can go ahead.

00:43:05.420 --> 00:43:06.003
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:43:32.203 --> 00:43:33.870
Yeah, I mean, this
is a great place to--

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:34.350
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:35.017
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:38.580
I was over across the street.

00:43:38.580 --> 00:43:40.650
And that's just a
hallway that we were in.

00:43:40.650 --> 00:43:43.510
So this is definitely
a lot more pleasing.

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:44.010
Yeah.

00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:46.177
AUDIENCE: What I find
difficult is I see something--

00:43:46.177 --> 00:43:48.350
PROFESSOR: Just make sure
you're balanced, level.

00:43:48.350 --> 00:43:51.140
AUDIENCE: Oh, yeah.

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:54.690
AUDIENCE: I keep
seeing pictures.

00:43:54.690 --> 00:43:57.740
But then when I film it, I
feel like it's not really

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:00.460
a video someone would
be interested in.

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:01.630
You know?

00:44:01.630 --> 00:44:04.650
PROFESSOR: Well, these are
just exercises at this point.

00:44:04.650 --> 00:44:09.150
AUDIENCE: This one--
isn't this [INAUDIBLE]??

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:12.480
PROFESSOR: Just fill the
frame with something white.

00:44:12.480 --> 00:44:17.040
AUDIENCE: Maybe zoom into
[INAUDIBLE] or something.

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:19.410
PROFESSOR: And make sure
that whatever you're

00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:23.220
filming the frame
with is going to be

00:44:23.220 --> 00:44:26.055
in your main light source,
under your main light source.

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:32.958
Actually, you got plenty
of light to choose from.

00:44:32.958 --> 00:44:34.288
AUDIENCE: It's mostly white.

00:44:34.288 --> 00:44:36.080
PROFESSOR: Just remember
what he was saying

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:38.260
about asymmetry in a frame.

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:41.730
It would be really interesting
if it's asymmetrical rather

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:43.642
than symmetrical.

00:44:49.380 --> 00:44:51.222
AUDIENCE: How can we
make this asymmetrical?

00:44:51.222 --> 00:44:52.930
AUDIENCE: I was just
thinking about that.

00:44:52.930 --> 00:44:55.430
PROFESSOR: You might want
to work on exposure too.

00:44:55.430 --> 00:44:58.300
You see, you have
the histogram up.

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:02.320
All those levels right there,
that's all your black level.

00:45:02.320 --> 00:45:04.480
You have hardly anything
in the highlights.

00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:05.980
The right of it
would be highlights.

00:45:05.980 --> 00:45:08.738
So if you want a
nice contrast, try

00:45:08.738 --> 00:45:10.030
to balance it out a little bit.

00:45:10.030 --> 00:45:12.590
So it's spread out
in a histogram.

00:45:12.590 --> 00:45:13.863
AUDIENCE: Like that?

00:45:13.863 --> 00:45:14.530
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:45:18.015 --> 00:45:20.250
When you're panning,
try to figure out

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:23.010
where your end shot will be.

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:24.180
And stay in that shot.

00:45:24.180 --> 00:45:28.810
So [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:28.810 --> 00:45:30.370
So instead of
shuffling your feet,

00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:33.670
you're just unwinding your body
into its natural place of rest.

00:45:33.670 --> 00:45:35.490
You know what I mean?

00:45:35.490 --> 00:45:38.040
So you're unwinding your
body rather than doing this.

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:41.430
And then it's like all
jittery, staggered.

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:44.250
So try to figure out what
your last frame will be.

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:47.772
And then you might want to
adjust the handle, whatever's

00:45:47.772 --> 00:45:48.480
more comfortable.

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:50.160
But just unwind your
body rather than

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:52.286
like shuffle with your feet.

00:45:52.286 --> 00:45:53.744
And you'll get a
much smoother pan.

00:45:56.980 --> 00:45:58.840
AUDIENCE: We did
exterior, interior.

00:45:58.840 --> 00:46:02.138
Portrait of a person
we haven't really done.

00:46:02.138 --> 00:46:02.680
What is that?

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:05.080
Like just zooming it
at someone's face?

00:46:05.080 --> 00:46:07.550
PROFESSOR: Yeah, it's just
finding a person, placing

00:46:07.550 --> 00:46:12.460
them interestingly in a frame.

00:46:12.460 --> 00:46:15.540
I would do like a medium
shot from waist up.

00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:17.400
Play around with like
focal length for that.

00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:19.860
You could do a wide
shot, medium, and a close

00:46:19.860 --> 00:46:20.940
up of the same person.

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:25.747
But again, with that, just
remember negative space.

00:46:25.747 --> 00:46:27.580
If you have the person
looking at something,

00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:30.810
just make sure that whatever
they're looking for, or looking

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:37.345
at, the negative space is in
front of their line of sight.

00:46:37.345 --> 00:46:39.470
AUDIENCE: So it doesn't
look like they're looking--

00:46:39.470 --> 00:46:41.700
PROFESSOR: Looking off
frame, exactly, yeah.

00:46:46.085 --> 00:46:48.350
How the screen's
divided into thirds.

00:46:48.350 --> 00:46:50.990
Try to keep the eyes somewhere
in that top third, that

00:46:50.990 --> 00:46:52.800
cross line.

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:54.350
AUDIENCE: OK.

